WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:03.940
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:07.999
This paper is really something else.

00:00:07.999 --> 00:00:14.180
Scientists at NVIDIA just came up with an
absolutely insane idea for video conferencing.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:19.320
Their idea is not to do what everyone else
is doing, which is, transmitting our video

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.740
to the person on the other end.

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:25.789
No, of course not, that would be too easy!

00:00:25.789 --> 00:00:31.259
What they do in this work, is take only the
first image from the video, and they throw

00:00:31.259 --> 00:00:34.040
away the entire video afterwards!

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:41.260
But before that, it stores a tiny bit of information
from it, which is, how our head is moving

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:45.210
over time, and how our expressions change.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:51.500
That is an absolutely outrageous idea… and
of course, we like those around here, so,

00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:52.530
does this work?

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:54.899
Well, let’s have a look.

00:00:54.899 --> 00:01:00.960
This is the input video, note that this is
not transmitted, only the first image and

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.780
some additional information, and the rest
of this video is discarded.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:10.990
And hold on to your papers, because this is
the output of the algorithm compared to the

00:01:10.990 --> 00:01:12.420
input video.

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:19.030
No, this is not some kind of misunderstanding,
nobody has copy-pasted the results there.

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:24.329
This is a near-perfect reconstruction of the
input, except that the amount of information

00:01:24.329 --> 00:01:29.970
we need to transmit through the network is
significantly less than with previous compression

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:31.140
techniques.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:32.140
How much less?

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:36.030
Well, you know what’s coming, so let’s
try it out!

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:42.920
Here is the output of the new technique, and
here is the comparison against H.264, a powerful

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:45.420
and commonly used video compression standard.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:52.700
Well, to our disappointment, the two seem
close, the new technique appears better, especially

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:56.969
around the glasses, but the rest is similar.

00:01:56.969 --> 00:02:02.750
And if you have been holding on to your papers
so far, now, squeeze that paper, because this

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:05.200
is not a reasonable comparison.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:11.769
And that is because the previous method was
allowed to transmit 6 to 12 times more information.

00:02:11.769 --> 00:02:17.670
Look, as we further decrease the data allowance
of the previous method, it still can transmit

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:23.390
more than twice as much information, and at
this point, there is no contest.

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:28.840
This bitrate would be unusable for any kind
of videoconferencing, while the new method

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:35.319
uses less than half as much information, and
still transmits a sharp and perfectly fine

00:02:35.319 --> 00:02:36.319
video.

00:02:36.319 --> 00:02:42.010
Overall, the authors report that their new
method is ten times more efficient.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:43.819
That is unreal.

00:02:43.819 --> 00:02:48.630
This is an excellent video reconstruction
technique, that much is clear.

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:52.220
And if it only did that, it would be a great
paper.

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:59.160
But this is not a great paper, this is an
absolutely amazing paper, so it does even

00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:00.160
more.

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:01.670
Much, much more!

00:03:01.670 --> 00:03:07.880
For instance, it can also rotate our head
and make a frontal video, can also fix potential

00:03:07.880 --> 00:03:14.230
framing issues by translating our head, and
transferring all of our gestures to a new

00:03:14.230 --> 00:03:15.230
model.

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:22.700
And, it is also evaluated well, so all of
these new features are tested in isolation.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:27.040
Look at these two previous methods trying
to frontalize the input video.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:32.549
One would think that it’s not even possible
to perform properly given how much these techniques

00:03:32.549 --> 00:03:38.069
are struggling with the task…until we look
at the new method.

00:03:38.069 --> 00:03:39.069
My goodness.

00:03:39.069 --> 00:03:46.300
There is some jumpiness in the neck movement
in the output video here, and some warping

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:50.760
issues here, but otherwise, very impressive
results.

00:03:50.760 --> 00:03:55.689
Now if you have been holding on to your papers
so far, now, squeeze that paper, because these

00:03:55.689 --> 00:04:01.549
previous methods are not some ancient papers
that were published a long time ago.

00:04:01.549 --> 00:04:02.630
Not at all!

00:04:02.630 --> 00:04:07.560
Both of them were published within the same
year as the new paper.

00:04:07.560 --> 00:04:09.739
How amazing is that.

00:04:09.739 --> 00:04:10.750
Wow.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:16.850
I really liked this page from the paper, which
showcases both the images and the mathematical

00:04:16.850 --> 00:04:20.700
measurements against previous methods side
by side.

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:25.480
There are many ways to measure how close two
videos are to each other, the up and down

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:32.010
arrows tell us whether the given quality metric
is subject to minimization or maximization,

00:04:32.010 --> 00:04:38.390
for instance, pixelwise errors are typically
minimized, so lesser is better, but we are

00:04:38.390 --> 00:04:41.700
to maximize the the peak signal to noise ratio.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:47.400
And the cool thing is that none of this matters
too much as soon as we insert the new technique,

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:50.580
which really outpaces all of these.

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:53.080
And we are still not done yet!

00:04:53.080 --> 00:04:58.610
So we said that the technique takes the first
image, reads the evolution of expressions

00:04:58.610 --> 00:05:04.850
and the head pose from the input video, and
then, it discards the entirety of the video,

00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:07.190
save for the first image.

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:12.700
The cool thing about this was that we could
pretend to rotate the head pose information,

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:17.830
and the result is that the head appears rotated
in the output image.

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:19.190
That was great.

00:05:19.190 --> 00:05:25.450
But what if we take the source image from
someone, and take this data, the driving keypoint

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:28.010
sequence from someone else?

00:05:28.010 --> 00:05:32.270
Well, what we get is, motion transfer.

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:33.940
Look!

00:05:33.940 --> 00:05:39.890
We only need one image of the target person,
and we can transfer all of our gestures to

00:05:39.890 --> 00:05:47.410
them, in a way that is significantly better
than most previous methods.

00:05:47.410 --> 00:05:55.850
Now, of course, not even this technique is
perfect, it still struggles a great deal in

00:05:55.850 --> 00:06:01.450
the presence of occluder objects, but still,
just the fact that this is possible feels

00:06:01.450 --> 00:06:05.210
like something straight out of a science fiction
movie.

00:06:05.210 --> 00:06:15.760
What 

00:06:15.760 --> 00:06:38.130
a 
time 

00:06:38.130 --> 00:07:01.240
to be alive!

00:07:01.240 --> 00:07:09.320
Thanks 
for watching and for your generous support,

00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:10.800
and I'll see you next time!

